{%- comment -%}
  The one place every launch date lives.

  Echoes the launch date of a variant as `YYYYMMDD`, or nothing when that
  variant has no launch date and is governed by stock alone.

  Walterin Prague sells two editions that launch a fortnight apart, so a date
  belongs to a VARIANT, not a product: the eBook on 23 October 2026, the
  printed book on 10 November. The tarot launches whole on 10 November.

  The table is `handle|match|YYYYMMDD`, where `match` is one of:
    - `*`         the whole product
    - `digital`   every variant that does not ship (the eBook)
    - `physical`  every variant that does ship (the printed book)
    - `sku:ABC`   one variant, by SKU

  **Never match on an option value or a variant title.** Those are translated:
  on /sk/ "Digital Interactive Edition" is Slovak, so a title match finds
  nothing and the whole gate silently disappears in one language. A SKU and
  requires_shipping are the same in every language. (This is the same reason
  the waitlist tag is built from the SKU.)

  A specific row always beats a `*` row, whatever order they appear in.

  Nothing else in the theme should hard-code a date. Cards, the buy column and
  the mobile bar all read it from here, so a change lands everywhere at once.

  Accepts: product (required), variant (optional — omit for a product-wide date)

  Usage:
    {%- capture launch_ts -%}
      {%- render 'launch-date', product: product, variant: variant -%}
    {%- endcapture -%}
    {%- assign launch_ts = launch_ts | strip -%}
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign rows = 'tarot|*|20261110,walterin-prague|digital|20261023,walterin-prague|physical|20261110' | split: ','
  assign result = ''

  for row in rows
    assign parts = row | split: '|'
    if parts[0] == product.handle
      if parts[1] == '*'
        comment
          A product-wide date is the fallback, so it never overwrites a date
          set for one edition.
        endcomment
        if result == blank
          assign result = parts[2]
        endif
      elsif parts[1] == 'digital'
        unless variant.requires_shipping
          assign result = parts[2]
        endunless
      elsif parts[1] == 'physical'
        if variant.requires_shipping
          assign result = parts[2]
        endif
      else
        assign want_sku = parts[1] | remove_first: 'sku:'
        if variant.sku != blank and variant.sku == want_sku
          assign result = parts[2]
        endif
      endif
    endif
  endfor

  echo result
-%}
